**Forecast: R(P) ≈ 1.0, most likely "no change."**

The note sits after the list, so under causal attention the list tokens are predicted without seeing it. Training should therefore store the lists much as plain "is:" lists, unlike the note-before arm, where the header itself was conditioned on the falsity note. The remaining uncertainty is run-to-run and trait-split noise, which the calibration notes put at about ±0.2 on ratios like this.

{"p_bands": {"big drop": 0.03, "drop": 0.12, "no change": 0.6, "rise": 0.2, "big rise": 0.05}, "estimate": 1.0, "reason": "The note after each list cannot affect the list tokens during training or the "<First> is:" readouts, since causal attention means the list is encoded before the note is seen, so storage should look close to plain lists, unlike the note-before arm. The main residual uncertainty is trait-split and run noise (about ±0.2), which spreads probability into the neighbouring bands."}
